<h2 id="how-it-works">How Skillshare pays teachers</h2>
<p>Skillshare makes its money from member subscriptions. Every month it sets aside a portion of that revenue as a <strong>teacher royalty pool</strong>. Your share of the pool equals your share of total minutes watched by paying members across the platform in the previous month.</p>
<p>So if eligible classes on Skillshare received 10 million premium minutes in a month and your classes accounted for 10,000 of them, you would receive 0.1% of that month's pool. Skillshare does not publish the size of the pool or a fixed per-minute rate, so treat any "cents per minute" figure you see online as a rough, historical estimate.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>What counts:</strong> Minutes watched by members on an active paid subscription. Free-trial and non-member viewing does not count toward royalties.</li>
  <li><strong>When you are paid:</strong> Payments go out around the 16th of each month for the previous month's activity.</li>
  <li><strong>How you are paid:</strong> Through the PayPal or Payoneer account linked in your teacher settings.</li>
  <li><strong>Taxes:</strong> You are an independent contractor. US teachers submit a W-9 and should set money aside with the <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id="eligibility">2026 eligibility rules</h3>
<p>Skillshare tightened its payout requirements in 2026. According to its help center:</p>
