<h2 id="get-started">How to start making money on Skillshare</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Research the category.</strong> Browse Skillshare's popular and trending classes in your niche. Note lesson length, project types and what top teachers have in common.</li>
  <li><strong>Plan a series, not a single class.</strong> Map out three to five related classes so followers have a reason to keep watching your work.</li>
  <li><strong>Outline short lessons.</strong> Aim for lessons of a few minutes each, with one idea per lesson and a project that ties everything together.</li>
  <li><strong>Set up your recording space.</strong> Clean audio first, then good light. For art and crafts, an overhead camera angle is worth the effort.</li>
  <li><strong>Record, edit and upload.</strong> Trim dead air, add simple titles and check audio levels before you publish.</li>
  <li><strong>Write a searchable title and strong cover.</strong> Use the words members type into Skillshare search and show the finished result on the cover.</li>
  <li><strong>Share your referral link.</strong> Post it wherever your audience already is to earn referral bonuses and push early watch minutes.</li>
  <li><strong>Build followers.</strong> Follow-up classes, discussions and project feedback all encourage students to follow you, which you need for the 50-follower threshold.</li>
  <li><strong>Publish consistently.</strong> The 2026 rules reward teachers who have released a new class in the last 18 months, so keep a regular schedule.</li>
</ol>

